{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"04","chapter_title":"Productivity and Workflow Automation","title":"AI-Assisted Email Drafting","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting ai-assisted email drafting to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-01","statement":"Write a specific email prompt that names the recipient, course, exact request, deadline, and reason"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-02","statement":"Run a verification pass on every fact (names, dates, the actual ask) before sending an AI-drafted message"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-03","statement":"Keep private data (student ID, grades, personal details) out of public AI tools"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-04","statement":"Keep a short log of which AI suggestions you accepted, modified, or rejected, and why"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation","cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/07/03"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# AI-Assisted Email Drafting\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting AI Assisted Email Drafting, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/07-04-ai-assisted-email-drafting.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for AI-Assisted Email Drafting: AI Assisted Email Drafting, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAn AI writing tool will happily produce a fluent, confident email that names the wrong professor, invents a deadline, or quietly drops the one request you actually needed to make. The fluency is the trap: a polished draft feels finished even when it is factually wrong. This section teaches you to use AI as a drafting accelerator while keeping yourself as the verifier, because in professional and academic email the cost of a confident error (a wrong name in the greeting, a fabricated date) is borne by you, not the tool.\n\nThe core competency is bounded delegation: giving the AI a specific enough prompt to produce something usable, then running a verification pass on every fact before the message leaves your outbox, and never feeding the tool private data it does not need.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write a specific email prompt that names the recipient, course, exact request, deadline, and reason.\n- Run a verification pass on every fact (names, dates, the actual ask) before sending an AI-drafted message.\n- Keep private data (student ID, grades, personal details) out of public AI tools.\n- Keep a short log of which AI suggestions you accepted, modified, or rejected, and why.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student types \"write an email to my professor\" into an AI tool and receives a grammatically perfect message addressed to \"Professor [Name]\" requesting \"the assignment extension we discussed.\" There was no discussion, the professor's name is missing, and there is no course or deadline. The draft is unusable not because the writing is bad but because the prompt gave the tool nothing real to work with, so it filled the gaps with plausible-sounding placeholders.\n\nThe fix runs in both directions: a specific prompt produces a usable draft, and a verification pass catches the inevitable remaining errors before they reach the recipient.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAI-assisted email rests on three moves: specify, verify, and bound.\n\n**Specify.** An AI tool produces output proportional to the constraints you give it. A prompt with the recipient, course, exact request, deadline, and reason yields a sendable draft; a vague prompt yields generic filler with placeholders. The prompt is where you do the thinking; the tool handles phrasing.\n\n**Verify.** AI tools generate text that is statistically likely, not necessarily true. They will confidently produce a name or date that sounds right but is wrong. So every factual element (the greeting name, the deadline, the course number, the specific ask) gets checked against a real source before sending. Fluency is not evidence of accuracy; the hallucination research literature treats confidently stated false names and dates as a structural property of language models, not an occasional bug (Huang et al., 2024), which is why verification is structural, not optional.\n\n**Bound.** Some AI tools retain or train on what you type, and the default varies by tool and account tier, so unless you have confirmed otherwise, treat the prompt as potentially stored. Identifiers and sensitive details (your student ID, grades, health specifics) should never go into the prompt. You add such items by hand, privately, only if the recipient actually needs them. The boundary is decided by the data's sensitivity, not by convenience.\n\n**Recent tools (2026).** Students draft email in concrete surfaces: Microsoft's \"Draft with Copilot\" and \"Help me write\" in Outlook, Google Gemini's \"Help me write\" in Gmail, and general assistants such as ChatGPT or Claude in the browser. Their data-use defaults differ by tier: consumer free and paid personal tiers typically use inputs for model training by default (opt-out required), while business, enterprise, and education tiers do not train on inputs by default. So the boundary check includes asking which tier you are in and what its data-use default is. Model names and retention defaults change frequently, so verify the current setting rather than trusting a remembered default.\n\nThe distinction students miss is between **polish** (smooth wording, which AI is good at) and **correctness plus appropriateness** (right facts, right boundary, right ask), which remain your responsibility.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An AI draft addressed to ’Professor Lee’ reads beautifully and names a deadline of ’this Friday.’ What does the smoothness of the writing tell you about whether those facts are correct?\" explanation=\"Fluency is produced by a language model predicting likely wording; it is independent of truth. A smooth draft can name the wrong professor or invent a date. Polish is never evidence of accuracy, so every fact still gets verified.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Nothing; smooth wording is unrelated to whether the name and date are true\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The facts are probably right, because the model would not write confidently otherwise\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The deadline is verified, since the model included a specific day\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The greeting is safe to trust because it uses a real-sounding title\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is a real email request taken from a vague AI prompt to a sendable draft.\n\n**Step 1, a weak prompt:**\n\n```\nWrite an email to my professor\n```\n\nThe AI returns a generic email with no course, no request, and a placeholder name. You cannot send it.\n\n**Step 2, a specific prompt:**\n\n```\nWrite a short, polite email to Prof. Lee for CAT 100 requesting a 2-day\nextension on the Module 7 lab, due Friday, because I was ill. Keep my\nstudent ID out of the message; I will add it later.\n```\n\n**Step 3, the draft, then your verification pass before sending:**\n\n```\nSubject: CAT 100 Module 7 Lab -- Extension Request\n\nDear Prof. Lee,                          [check: real name is Lee, correct]\nI am a student in your CAT 100 section. I was ill this week and\nwould like to request a two-day extension on the Module 7 lab,\ncurrently due Friday.                    [check: deadline Friday, correct]\nThank you for considering this.\nBest,\n[Your name]                              [fill in; ID added only if she asks, privately]\n```\n\nThree decisions did the work:\n\n1. **Specifics in the prompt** (recipient, course, exact ask, deadline, reason) so the draft is usable.\n2. **A verification pass** confirming the name and date are real before the email leaves your outbox.\n3. **Privacy boundary**: the student ID never went into the public AI tool; it is added by hand only if needed.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTreat the AI as a drafter and yourself as the editor of record. Before prompting, decide what facts the tool needs and what sensitive data it must not see. After drafting, verify every fact before you click send.\n\nA sendable AI-drafted email passes three checks:\n\n1. **Specificity check:** Did the prompt include the recipient, course, exact request, deadline, and reason?\n2. **Verification check:** Has every name, date, and claim in the draft been confirmed against a real source?\n3. **Boundary check:** Is all private or identifying data kept out of the AI tool and added by hand only where needed?\n\nIf any fact is unverified, the email is a draft, not a message ready to send.\n\nWalk the full path from a useless prompt to a sendable message, predicting each move before it is revealed.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Take an AI email request from vague prompt to sendable draft\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You type ’write an email to my professor’ and get a polished message addressed to ’Professor [Name]’ about ’the extension we discussed.’ What is the first fix?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Send it; the writing is clean\",\"Rewrite the prompt to name the recipient, course, exact request, deadline, and reason\",\"Ask the AI to make it longer\",\"Add your student ID so the tool has context\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Specify first. A vague prompt forces the tool to invent placeholders. Naming Prof. Lee, CAT 100, a 2-day extension on the Module 7 lab due Friday, and the reason gives it something real to phrase.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The specific prompt returns a clean draft. Before sending, which move comes next?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Click send; the prompt was detailed\",\"Run a verification pass on every fact: the name, the deadline, the course, the ask\",\"Forward it to a classmate to proofread the grammar\",\"Add more polite phrases\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Verify. Check that the professor is actually Lee, the lab is truly due Friday, and the request matches what you need. Fluency is not accuracy, so each fact is confirmed against a real source.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The draft is verified. Your student ID is needed only if she asks. Where does it go?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Paste it into the AI prompt so the draft is complete\",\"Add it by hand, privately, only if the recipient requests it\",\"Put it in the subject line for clarity\",\"Email it to yourself first\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Bound the data. The ID never enters the public AI tool; you add it by hand only if needed. Specify, verify, bound: the three moves that turn a draft into a sendable message.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The prompt did the thinking, the verification pass caught the errors, and the boundary kept sensitive data out of the tool. That is bounded delegation: the AI accelerates the wording while you stay the editor of record.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most dangerous misreading is trusting fluency. A smooth, confident draft can still name the wrong instructor or invent a date; smoothness is not accuracy. Always verify the facts.\n\nA second misreading is over-sharing in the prompt. Pasting your full record \"so the AI has context\" hands sensitive data to a tool that, depending on its tier and data-use default, may retain or train on it. Give the tool only what the email itself requires.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply this to a recommendation-letter request, which is higher-stakes than an extension. You prompt: \"Write a polite email to Dr. Ramirez asking if she would write a recommendation letter for the summer research program, due June 15; remind her I took her CAT 100 in spring and earned an A; offer to send my resume.\" The tool drafts a courteous message.\n\nNow verify: is the professor's name spelled Ramirez? Is the deadline really June 15? Did you actually take her course in spring? Each is a fact the tool will assert confidently whether or not it is true, so each is checked against your records and the program page. The boundary move: you do not paste your transcript into the tool; you simply reference the grade in the prompt and attach the resume yourself. The walkthrough shows that the higher the stakes, the more the verification pass matters, because a wrong name in a recommendation request is a self-inflicted wound.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader reads an AI draft suspiciously, looking specifically for the confident facts that have not been checked. The smoother the draft, the more deliberately they verify, because polish hides errors well.\n\nA strong reader of an AI-drafted email can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which facts in this draft did the tool generate, and which have I confirmed against a real source?\n2. What did the prompt include or omit that shaped the draft's quality?\n3. What private data did I keep out of the tool, and how does the recipient still get what they need?\n4. If a fact were wrong, who pays the cost, and have I checked that fact?\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting\", \"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is the email plus a short critique log marking each AI suggestion as accepted, modified, or rejected, with the reason. It is strong when a reader can see not just the final message but the judgment behind it: which facts were verified and which suggestions were turned down.\n\nA useful log entry sounds like this: \"Accepted the AI's subject line; modified the greeting after confirming the professor goes by Dr. not Prof.; rejected the AI's offer to 'attach my full transcript' because the request does not need it and the tool should not see grade data.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove AI drafting from email to a cover letter for a job application. Specify, verify, and bound all transfer, but the boundary tightens: a cover letter may tempt you to paste the full job posting and your resume into the tool. The adaptation is to check the employer's and the AI tool's policies, since some application systems forbid AI-generated text and some AI tools retain inputs. The three moves stay; what adapts is the stricter privacy and policy environment around hiring.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI-assisted email drafting packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/07-04-ai-assisted-email-drafting-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"07.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the specify-verify-bound rule to a new setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the draft.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 07.04. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your email passes the specificity, verification, and boundary checks: the prompt was specific, every fact is confirmed, and no private data went into the AI tool.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Huang, L., et al. (2024). A survey on hallucination in large language models: Principles, taxonomy, challenges, and open questions. ACM TOIS. https://doi.org/10.1145/3703155\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting","cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation"],"stem":"An AI email draft to your professor names her 'Dr. Smith' but your professor is Dr. Lee, and it invents office hours. What is the right move before sending?","options":["Send it; the AI usually gets names right","Read and verify the recipient name, dates, and any claimed facts, then fix them","Forward it to yourself first","Add more exclamation points so it sounds friendly"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI does not know your real professor or schedule. Always check names, dates, and facts before the email leaves your outbox.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_send_unedited_ai","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting"],"stem":"You want to ask for a two-day extension. Which prompt gives the AI enough to draft a usable email?","options":["Write an email to my professor","Write a short, polite email to Prof. Lee for CAT 100 requesting a 2-day extension on the Module 7 lab, due Friday, because I was ill","Make it sound smart","Email about my class"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The specific prompt names the recipient, course, exact request, deadline, and reason. A one-line prompt yields a generic draft you cannot use.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_no_specifics_in_prompt","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-01","cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting"],"stem":"Which opening best fits an email to a professor about a grade question?","options":["Hey can u bump my grade thx","Subject: CAT 100 Lab 3 grade question -- Dear Dr. Lee, I have a question about the rubric points on Lab 3","yo prof whats up with this grade","TO WHOM IT MAY CONCERN: I DEMAND A REVIEW"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A professor audience needs a clear subject line, a named greeting, and a specific, calm request. Casual or aggressive tone undercuts the ask.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_tone_mismatch","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_ai_assisted_email_drafting_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_assisted_email_drafting"],"stem":"You ask a public AI tool to draft an email about a missed exam. What should you keep out of the prompt?","options":["The fact that you missed an exam","Your full student ID number and a private medical note; use a placeholder like [STUDENT ID] instead","The course name","The professor's title"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sensitive identifiers and medical details should not go into a public tool. Draft with a placeholder, then fill the private value in yourself.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_leak_private_info","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-04-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_send_unedited_ai","pattern":"send_ai_draft_unread","description":"Sending an AI-generated email without checking facts, names, dates, or tone for the real recipient","trigger":"learner pastes the AI draft and hits send, keeping a wrong professor name or invented office hours","feedback":"Read every AI draft before sending. Verify the recipient name, dates, and any claimed facts; the AI does not know your real details.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_no_specifics_in_prompt","pattern":"vague_prompt_to_ai","description":"Giving the AI a one-line prompt with no recipient, purpose, or facts, then expecting a usable email","trigger":"learner types 'write an email to my professor' with no course, request, or deadline","feedback":"Put the specifics in the prompt: who it is to, the course, the exact ask, and any dates. A vague prompt yields a generic, unusable draft.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_tone_mismatch","pattern":"casual_tone_for_formal_email","description":"Keeping an overly casual or overly stiff AI tone that does not fit a professor or employer audience","trigger":"learner sends 'Hey can u bump my grade thx' to a professor","feedback":"Match tone to audience. For a professor or employer, use a clear subject line, a greeting with their name, a specific request, and a sign-off.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_4_leak_private_info","pattern":"pasting_private_data_into_ai","description":"Pasting sensitive personal data such as a student ID or grades into a public AI tool to draft an email","trigger":"learner pastes their full student ID number and medical note into the chat prompt","feedback":"Keep private identifiers out of the prompt. Use a placeholder like [STUDENT ID] and fill it in yourself after the draft returns.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"fb01df953b93860e87623213b011176eeb71fb3ebb7272daf5cecaeae8a7bf54","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}